          "text": "{\"translation_en\":null,\"larger_context_summary\":\"On the eve of their intended marriage, Edward Rochester addresses his fiancée, Jane Eyre, who has been troubled by ominous dreams and anxieties about their future. He dismisses her fears as “visionary woe,” recalls her earlier statement that love makes the prospect of living with him glorious, and presses her to repeat the avowal. Jane believes they are free to marry, while Rochester is concealing the existence of his living wife, Bertha Mason—a fact that will shortly prevent the wedding. His pleasure in Jane’s words therefore sits within a relationship whose apparent security is gravely compromised by information she does not possess.\",\"interpretive_commentary\":\"The highlighted words occur inside Rochester’s quotation—or close revoicing—of Jane’s earlier speech, so the immediate speaker is not newly declaring his own love. Within the recalled sentence, Jane presents her love as a fact about her emotional state and as the reason she welcomes the hope of living with Edward. That explanatory structure makes a strongly truth-conditional reading especially persuasive. Although the avowal has major relational consequences and Rochester treats it as precious confirmation, it does not itself promise marriage, continued cohabitation, or any future action; those implications belong to the situation rather than to its performative force. Nor does “clear and soft” indicate an involuntary outburst or reflexive expressive discharge: Rochester characterizes the words as solemn, deliberate, and intelligible. The revoiced form is analytically valuable because it shows how another participant receives the declaration—as something that can be remembered, affirmed, denied, and requested again—while preserving Jane’s original first-person emotional claim.\"}"
